## 5. Edge Cases in Presentation

| Case | Handling |
|:--|:--|
| **Δf = 0 (identical decoded freqs)** | Category `UNISON (exact)`. Never compute a beat period (would be ∞). Optional gloss: "infinite-sustain unison — no amplitude modulation." Ratio = 1.000, JI 1:1, error 0. |
| **Δf > ~40 Hz** | Label `DIFFERENCE TONE`, not "beat". Additionally run §1 mapping on Δf itself and display it as a pitch (e.g., 440 vs 660 → Δf = 220 Hz = A3). If Δf < 20 Hz it cannot be a tone — keep beat categories. |
| **Δf in ultrasonic/infrasonic band** | If both f₁,f₂ ultrasonic, a Δf may fall in the audible band (e.g., 22 000 vs 21 980 → Δf = 20 Hz ROUGHNESS, but nothing audible at the carriers). Print: "carriers inaudible; difference frequency hypothetical unless nonlinear demodulation exists." |
| **Negative cents / rounding** | Report signed cents in [−50, +50). Never round e.g. −49.7 ¢ across the boundary to "+50.3 of the next note" — re-express as the neighbor note instead if |cents| ≥ 50 after rounding of f. At exactly ±50.0 ¢, report both adjacent spellings. |
| **Note names < 20 Hz** | Allowed: MIDI numbers go negative (e.g., 8 Hz → C−1 −37.6 ¢; 5.5 Hz → F−2 +13.7 ¢). Always band-labeled `INFRASONIC`. Octave numbers below −1 are legal in scientific pitch notation under this convention (floor(m/12)−1). |
| **f above MIDI 127** | MIDI note number is a mapping device, not a limit — values > 127 (e.g., 22 kHz → MIDI 136.7 → F10) are fine; label ULTRASONIC. |
| **No close JI interval** | If best |cents error| > 50 ¢, print "— (no close JI interval)" instead of a forced interval name. |
| **Compound ratios > 2:1** | Show raw ratio plus octave-reduced match, e.g. `3.01 ≈ 2×3:2 (twelfth, +2.6 ¢)`. |
| **Inharmonicity warning** | Membrane/plate mode ratios (1.594, 2.136, …) are not integer harmonics; any "note" rendering of overtones is nominal. |

